# PHASE 0 — Upgrade the stack

Blocking. Nothing in Phases 1–4 starts until this is green.

## Target versions

| Package | From | To |
|---|---|---|
| `langchain` | 1.1.5 | ^1.5.4 |
| `@langchain/core` | 1.1.4 | ^1.2.3 |
| `@langchain/langgraph` | 1.0.x | ^1.4.7 |
| `@langchain/langgraph-checkpoint` | 0.1.1 | ^1.1.3 |

## Verified ground truth

Established by diffing every exported symbol in every `.d.ts` across all four package tarballs, plus reading the monorepo changesets:

- **Zero exported symbols removed** in any of the four packages. No subpaths removed. `langchain` adds 203 symbols, `langgraph` 125, `core` 11 new subpaths.
- **No `Major Changes` changelog entries** for `langchain` or `@langchain/core` anywhere in the 1.x line; none for `langgraph` after 1.0.0.
- `checkpoint` 1.0.0 is a version-alignment release only — its changeset reads "updates the package for compatibility with LangGraph v1.0." The substantive breaks (`pending_sends`, `Checkpoint.writes`) were in **0.1.0**, already behind us.
- `BaseCheckpointSaver` abstract surface is **identical**: `getTuple`, `list`, `put`, `putWrites`, `deleteThread`. 1.1.3 adds `toJSON()` and `getDeltaChannelHistory()`, both with default implementations. Custom checkpointers compile and run unmodified.

**Conclusion: your code compiles. That is not the same as behaving identically.** The gotchas below are where the risk actually lives.

## Gotchas — ranked by real risk

### G1 — `afterModel` tool routing changed. Highest risk; lands directly on this package.

`langchain@1.2.34` changed `createAgent`'s tool-call routing when `afterModel` middleware is present. Previously, `afterModel` middleware **silently forced `version: "v1"` behaviour** (all tool calls to a single `ToolNode` via `Promise.all`) regardless of the `version` setting. Now `version: "v2"` — **the default** — dispatches each tool call as a separate `Send` task.

The changeset is explicitly labelled a breaking change for `version: "v2"` + `afterModel` users.

**Action:** grep the repo for `afterModel` **before** bumping. Any agent using it changes tool-execution topology on upgrade. Pin `version: "v1"` on those agents to preserve current behaviour, then evaluate v2 deliberately as a separate decision. `version?: "v1" | "v2"` exists on `CreateAgentParams` in both 1.1.5 and 1.5.4, so the escape hatch is available on both sides of the bump.

### G2 — `langsmith` jumps 0.3 → 0.5+. Unverified; budget time.

`@langchain/core@1.1.4` depends on `langsmith ^0.3.64`. `@langchain/core@1.2.4` depends on `>=0.5.0 <1.0.0`. That is a 0.x jump across two minors, and 0.x minors are semver-breaking by definition.

This is the one surface I could **not** verify — no JS changelog published at the expected path. If you use LangSmith directly (custom client, tracing config, evaluators, run trees), verify independently before committing. If you only consume tracing via env vars, risk is low.

### G3 — It is one decision, not four.

`langchain@1.1.5` pins core **exactly**: `"@langchain/core": "1.1.4"`, no caret. It also carries `@langchain/langgraph` and `@langchain/langgraph-checkpoint` as regular `dependencies`, not peers. Bumping `langchain` drags all three regardless.

`langchain@1.5.4` requires core `^1.2.3` — `^1.2.0` is **not** sufficient.

**Do not stage these bumps.** Staging buys nothing and creates intermediate states nobody tested.

### G4 — zod floor moves.

`@langchain/langgraph` peer goes `^3.25.32 || ^4.1.0` → `^3.25.32 || ^4.2.0`. Fine on zod 3. On zod 4.0/4.1 you must move to 4.2+.

### G5 — New transitive dependencies.

`langgraph` 1.4.x adds `@langchain/protocol` and `@standard-schema/spec`, drops `uuid`. `core` 1.2.x drops `ansi-styles`, `camelcase`, `decamelize`, `uuid`, adds `@standard-schema/spec`. Only matters if you have lockfile review, vendoring, or licence-audit gates.

### G6 — Deprecations (not removals; all still functional through 1.x).

`streamLog`, `streamEvents` v1, `RunnableWithMessageHistory` deprecated in core 1.2.x. `createReactAgent` in langgraph deprecated since 1.0.0-alpha in favour of `langchain`. Inspect for usage; schedule migration separately.

### G7 — Import paths. Correction to an earlier draft of this plan.

`langchain` has **no `./agents` subpath export**. Published subpaths in 1.1.5 are `.`, `./chat_models/universal`, `./hub`, `./hub/node`, `./load`, `./load/serializable`, `./storage/*`. 1.5.4 adds `./browser` and `./tools`.

Every middleware is exported from the **root**:

```ts
import { createAgent, createMiddleware, todoListMiddleware,
         summarizationMiddleware, anthropicPromptCachingMiddleware } from 'langchain';
```

`import ... from 'langchain/agents/middleware'` will fail. An earlier draft got this wrong.

### G8 — Package.json discipline.

LangChain packages stay in `peerDependencies` with **caret ranges, never pins** — a pin makes this package unadoptable by anyone on a different minor. Mirror into `devDependencies` so build and tests resolve. Single shared copy of `@langchain/core` is non-negotiable; two copies breaks `instanceof` on messages and tools.

## What the upgrade unlocks

New in `langchain@1.5.4` and relevant here:

- `toolErrorMiddleware`, `bedrockPromptCachingMiddleware`, `providerToolSearchMiddleware`
- `createSubagentTransformer` / `SubagentRunStream` — **streaming observability only.** Surfaces nested named agents on `run.subagents` when children are built with `createAgent({ name })`. It is *not* a delegation mechanism; there is still no `task` tool and no subagent middleware. Phase 3 does not shrink. It does get free nested-run visibility.
- Still **no filesystem middleware** at any version. Phase 2 is yours regardless.

